xpf (0.0.0) unstable; urgency=medium
  * Cluster/config: an omitted `redundancy-group ip-monitoring
    global-weight` now defaults to 255, the Junos default (#16667).
    Previously it compiled to 0, so aggregate mode deducted nothing
    once the cumulative failure sum crossed `global-threshold` and an
    unweighted target inherited zero debt in independent mode — the
    redundancy group never demoted while status reported it healthy.
    An explicit `global-weight 0` still means no demotion, and a later
    folded block omitting the global no longer resets an earlier
    explicit value.
  * IPsec host-input fence: the 5 ms supervisor reconcile no longer
    acquires applySem on settled boxes without a route-based VPN
    (#16777). With a SAFE census, a CLOSING permit and an ACKed
    overlay but no committed divert, every tick took the semaphore
    only to find nothing to open, parking behind whole commits. The
    reconciler now returns before acquiring in exactly that settled
    state; non-SAFE, un-ACKed, removal-pending and divert-active
    states are unchanged.
  * Shim verifier provenance/headroom (#16701): the gate verdict is
    now schema 3 and records provenance.kernel_release (the uname -r
    the processed-insn count was measured on); freshness and release
    retention gates fail closed without it. `xpfd verify-dataplane`
    now prints the processed count, headroom, floor and running
    kernel and enforces the 15% floor on the target kernel (new
    exit 4 below floor, 5 unmeasured; no guest-side override), so a
    guest kernel charging more than the build host (#15645, #15854:
    +3.8-6.1%) refuses early instead of hiding a later REJECT.
  * Flowexport/docs: `pkg/flowexport/README.md` now documents the
    post-#15946/#16126/#16303 contract (#16763): one sampling decision
    at SESSION_OPEN (`SampledForOpen` via the single
    `flowExportOpenCallback`), tracker active/inactive semantics with
    the 60s/15s defaults, the 1s live-counter poll feeding tracked
    flows, and that absent-open or inactive-expired closes export
    nothing. The per-SESSION_CLOSE sampler wording is corrected in the
    README and in `ipfix.go`, and a docsref gate pins the contract.

  * Config/BGP: leaves chained under a valueless BGP flag
    (`passive`, `remove-private`, `log-updown`) are now validated at
    the enclosing BGP container on strict commit (#16749), exactly as
    the compilers expand them. Previously
    `set protocols bgp group G passive hold-time 1` committed clean,
    rendered `neighbor ... timers 1 1`, failed frr-reload.py, and
    degraded every later FRR reload; chained unknown keywords now
    also draw the #10707 advisory. The renderer additionally omits a
    `timers` line for a hold-time of 1/2 (or above 65535) arriving on
    the tolerant path, with a warning, instead of poisoning reloads.
    Valid chained values (for example `passive hold-time 9`) commit
    and apply as before.
  * Cluster/session-sync: pre-auth logging on the sync listener is
    now bounded (#16748). The accept loop's "peer connected" line is
    Debug instead of Info (it fired before authentication, so a
    PSK-less host on the sync segment drove one journal line per
    connection), and the "auth handshake failed" warning is
    rate-limited to about one per second with a new
    PreAuthHandshakeFailed sync-stats counter recording every
    failure. Post-auth connection logging is unchanged.
  * Config/firewall: the single-rate `firewall policer`
    `burst-size-limit` floor is now the shared one-frame floor of
    1514 bytes (#16714), unified with the three-color leaves
    (#16610): values 1500..1513 are rejected at strict commit and
    warned on tolerant load. Previously 1500 committed clean (the
    #10310 L3-byte floor) while the userspace meter charges the
    full Ethernet frame (1500-byte MTU payload + 14-byte header),
    so every full-size frame was discarded at any rate. A burst at
    exactly 1514 still commits; tagged (1518) or jumbo traffic
    needs a proportionally larger burst.
  * Build/test: `make test-go` now races every Race/Concurrent-named
    Go test in pkg/ and cmd/ via a property leg in test-race-dp
    (#16715). Previously only three name-filtered legs ran under
    -race, so the SNMP #13127 and syslog #9916 race cells (and ~100
    others) passed without the race detector. The pkg/dataplane
    #9045 canary again parses the real race line (not the -list
    pre-check), and a new pkg/docsref census fails when any
    race-named test falls outside every -race leg.
  * Configstore: the pre-commit diff summary (CLI/gRPC commit) no longer
    allocates a full (lines+1)x(lines+1) LCS table (#16718). At 12k set
    lines that was ~1.1 GiB inside xpfd on every commit (2 GiB at 16k,
    OOM risk on the 4 GiB appliance). The diff now trims the common
    prefix/suffix and computes the exact LCS length with two DP rows,
    falling back to multiset counts only for an oversized middle section
    (full rewritten/reordered block above ~4M cells). A 50k-line config
    with a one-line edit now summarises in well under 10 MiB; order- and
    duplicate-sensitive counts are unchanged for ordinary edits.
  * API/auth: runtime enforcement of api-auth `expires` between AuthConfig
    rebuilds is now pinned by discriminating tests (#16729, #10826).
    Previously the two production `credentialExpiryActive` terms — the
    Basic check in checkAuthorizationIdentity and the API-key check in
    matchAPIKeyIdentityWithExpiry — could each be deleted with the whole
    pkg/api suite staying green, because the only "expired credential"
    cell (#13241) installed a test resolver that reimplemented the expiry
    check itself. New tests drive Basic, X-API-Key and Bearer through
    authMiddleware and the principalFrom credential row through the
    production default resolver, each with unexpired and no-expiry
    controls; deleting either production term now turns a named cell red.
    The #13241 memo cell is documented as covering memo invalidation
    only. No production behaviour changes.
  * Config: packed hierarchical `security flow tcp-session
    time-wait-state session-timeout N;` / `session-ageout;` (and the
    half-close leaves, including multi-leaf packed runs) now compile
    to the same `TCPSessionConfig` fields as the braced spelling
    (#16662). Previously the time-wait-state compiler loop read only
    the node's Children, so the packed spelling passed strict commit,
    displayed as configured, and silently kept the default TIME_WAIT
    close-window handling.
  * Config/feeds: strict commit now rejects a dynamic-address feed-server
    whose URL/hostname host is a non-public IP literal (private, loopback,
    link-local, CGNAT, benchmarking, ULA, transition/special-purpose)
    that the feed SSRF destination policy would refuse at fetch time
    (#16656). Previously such a server committed clean, never fetched,
    and — once referenced — froze policy publication at previous-good
    (fresh boot: default-deny). The destination predicate is now shared
    in pkg/config (FeedDestinationBlocked) between the compiler gate and
    the pkg/feeds runtime guard. A new per-server
    `allow-destination <prefix>` leaf (repeatable) opts that server's
    covered destinations into the runtime allowlist; without it the
    fetch stays default-deny. Public hostnames and literals, and the
    plaintext-http gate, behave as before.
  * NAT64: ICMPv6 Packet-Too-Big translated to ICMPv4
    Fragmentation-Needed now subtracts 28 (not 20) when the packet
    in error carries a Fragment Header, per RFC 7915 §5.2 (#16666).
    A translated fragment is `v4_len + 28` (20-byte header delta
    plus the 8-byte Fragment Header); the old unconditional -20
    advertised a Next-Hop MTU 8 bytes too large, so every DF-clear
    retry still exceeded the IPv6 bottleneck and PMTUD never
    converged. Unfragmented quotes keep the -20 adjustment.
  * API/TLS: fix AB-BA lock inversion that could permanently wedge
    config apply (#16706). HTTPS leg recovery after a certificate
    (re)install ran under tlsCertificateMu and took lifeMu, while an
    HTTPS leg rebuild held lifeMu and took tlsCertificateMu in
    buildHTTPSServer; an overlapping hourly monitor reinstall and
    apply deadlocked, freezing every later commit until restart.
    Recovery now runs after tlsCertificateMu is released, so the
    only nesting is lifeMu -> tlsCertificateMu.
  * Routing/VRF: instance-to-VRF-device mapping is now unconditional
    (#16664). `diagcmd.VRFDeviceName` previously returned a
    `vrf-`-prefixed routing-instance name unchanged (#2143), while the
    daemon and FRR always realise instance N as `vrf-`+N, so instance
    `vrf-X` silently targeted sibling instance `X`'s VRF (or a
    nonexistent device) for ping/traceroute, DDNS SO_BINDTODEVICE
    egress and HA routing-readiness FRR queries. The mapping now lives
    in `routing.VRFDeviceName` (single source of truth, prepends
    always); diagcmd, frr status queries and DDNS resolve through it,
    and an instance named `vrf-mgmt` maps to `vrf-vrf-mgmt`, never the
    management VRF.
  * Config/load: `ClassifyLoadContent` now routes a body to flat
    replay only when a flat-verb line appears at brace depth 0
    (#16698). Previously any uncommented flat-verb line flipped the
    whole body to flat replay, so the box's own `show configuration`
    output for a hierarchically authored unquoted event-options
    change-configuration command — rendered bare as
    `set system host-name pwned;` inside `commands { ... }` — failed
    `load override` and `load merge` at line 1. The show-to-load
    restore round-trip works again for such configs; flat scripts
    (verb lines at depth 0, including after a quoted brace) are
    unchanged, and mixed bodies still fail loud in flat replay.
  * Config/chassis-cluster: the four raw-AST commit gates (monitor
    weight #6588, RG/node identity #5694, no-argument RG statement
    arity #6588, gratuitous-arp-count #5695) now resolve the cluster
    body through normalizedClusterNode — the same view compileChassis
    reads — instead of walking only `chassis { cluster { } }`
    children (#16663). Previously the `chassis cluster { }`
    brace-top-level and packed spellings #6672 made compile bypassed
    every gate: a typo'd interface-monitor weight committed as weight
    0 (link-down deducts nothing, the RG never demotes), duplicate
    monitors, malformed redundancy-group / node ids and
    `preempt delay` all committed while the container spelling
    rejected them. Strict commit now rejects identically in every
    spelling; the tolerant load path warns as before (#1960).
  * Daemon/bootstrap: a failed fxp0 `.network` write now refuses the
    bootstrap lifeline rename (#16682). writeBootstrapLifelineNetwork
    laundered a failed atomic write into (false, nil), so bootstrap
    still wrote the .link, renamed/cycled the management NIC to fxp0
    and reloaded with no fxp0 .network to re-address it — console-only
    on a storage fault. The writer now returns the write error and
    setupBootstrapLifeline refuses before the .link write, the rename
    and the reload, exactly as for an observation failure.
  * Sessions/HA display: unresolvable session HA ownership now renders
    "unknown" on every surface (#16697). Previously `sessionHAActive`
    returned known=false but GetSessions stamped `HaActive=false` on a
    plain bool wire field, so the remote CLI and the console peer block
    guessed "Backup" (on a no-RETH cluster, for every session) while
    local rows said "unknown". Added additive tri-state `ha_state`
    ("Active"/"Backup"/"unknown") to gRPC SessionEntry (field 31) and
    REST SessionEntry, stamped at all four gRPC sites and in REST;
    renderers prefer it and treat empty (pre-#16697 peer) as unknown.
    Active/Backup rows unchanged.
  * Daemon/ip-monitoring: HA gating is now re-evaluated on
    event-delivered RG failovers (#16690). Previously the probe set
    and overlay publish gate were only refreshed when the periodic
    reconcile itself observed the rg-state edge, which the cluster /
    VRRP event handlers had already consumed, so after a normal
    failover the new owner ran no HA-gated probes and kept its old
    publish gate (and the new standby kept both). The event paths
    now schedule the gating reconcile off the event goroutine.
  * Daemon/HA: the #15258 sync-hold mirror refresh is now an edge
    producer on every path (#16692). Previously, in legacy
    no-private-rg-election mode, watchVRRPEvents never refreshed the
    mirror, so the preempt MASTER event ReleaseSyncHold itself triggers
    was judged against syncHold=true — no rg_active write and no RETH
    services — and the later handleClusterEvent/reconcile refresh
    discarded the transition its own SetSyncHold produced, so neither
    path saw tr.Changed. The preempt-failback owner served no RG-scoped
    Kea DHCP and re-initiated no RG-anchored IPsec SAs until an
    unrelated RG edge or commit. Now watchVRRPEvents and
    watchClusterEvents refresh the mirror scoped to the event's RG at
    the top of per-event handling and merge the refresh transition into
    the handler's tr; handleClusterEvent re-refreshes scoped so direct
    callers are covered; reconcileRGState ORs the pass-wide refresh
    into each RG's tr. Scoping prevents an event for one RG from
    consuming another RG's pending release edge.
  * Daemon/bootstrap: the #9615 refused-target bootstrap safe state
    now has a commit exit (#16665). Previously bootstrap refused
    plain `commit` and the #6707 gate refused `commit confirmed`
    on the very refused target that caused the bootstrap, so the
    only way out was a reboot. The refused branch now records its
    provenance: a plain `commit` of a corrected candidate is
    allowed (the refused target is already the persisted committed
    config) and the #6707 gate is skipped in that state (a timeout
    returns to the same safe state). Generic bootstrap is unchanged.
  * Config/NPTv6: `NPTv6OverlapConflict` now skips non-NPTv6 rules
    (`!rule.IsNPTv6`), mirroring `compileNPTv6` and
    `buildNptv6Snapshots` (#16674). Previously every static-NAT rule
    with a non-empty match/then was treated as NPTv6, so two ordinary
    IPv6 block static-NAT rules (`then static-nat prefix`, #3031)
    with a /64 inside a /48 — resolved by longest prefix in the
    helper's static table — passed strict commit but aborted every
    later apply in the #4960 pre-pass naming an NPTv6 overlap, even
    with zero NPTv6 rules. True NPTv6/NPTv6 overlaps still reject.
  * LLDP: Stop()/re-Apply can no longer hang on a silent link (#16669).
    The RX goroutine previously parked in a bare Recvfrom on the AF_PACKET
    socket and teardown relied on shutdown()+close() to wake it; shutdown
    is ENOTCONN on a connectionless AF_PACKET socket and close does not
    wake an in-flight Recvfrom on Linux, so an LLDP config change, a
    disable, or daemon shutdown blocked until a frame arrived (forever on
    silent `interface all` members), wedging the whole config-apply
    pipeline behind applySem and forcing the TimeoutStopSec SIGKILL path.
    recv now waits in ppoll on the RX fd plus a per-session wake eventfd,
    Stop runs cancel -> signal -> wg.Wait() -> close (in that order, so
    no recv is ever in flight on a closed/fd-reused descriptor), and
    rxLoop re-checks the context after every successful recv.
  * gRPC ShowText: `flow-statistics` now admits its full v4+v6
    session-table walk through the shared session-walk limiter
    (AcquireCtx on the ShowText context) and returns walk failures
    as errors (#16677). Previously it walked on
    context.Background() outside the MaxConcurrentSessionWalks
    budget — so a disconnecting client did not stop it and
    concurrent view callers could stack unbounded walks — and it
    discarded the walk error, printing "Current sessions: 0" on
    failure. Over-cap calls now fail with ResourceExhausted and a
    failed walk surfaces instead of a clean zero.
  * Daemon: a fail-closed boot of a committed firewall no longer treats
    the main-table default-route NIC (the WAN, since fxp0 lives in
    vrf-mgmt) as the management lifeline (#16671). The persisted
    lifeline record is never overwritten from route detection once a
    config has committed, the WAN is not renamed to fxp0, and the
    cold-boot live-address fences derive management only from the
    persisted record, the management leaf, and management-class names
    — so WAN addresses are fenced instead of exposing host services
    (sshd/IKE) on the untrust side until a compilable commit.
  * Daemon/bootstrap: the first-commit `commit confirmed` rollback to
    bootstrap now withdraws the abandoned config's Router Advertisement
    senders (lifetime-0 goodbye), stops the Kea DHCP server units via
    `Apply(nil)`, and stops the DHCP relay (#16678). Previously
    `enterBootstrapMode` retired only networkd/FRR/dataplane/cluster
    state, so the box kept advertising itself as an IPv6 default router
    and kept leasing DHCP with itself as gateway on the abandoned
    config's segments while forwarding nothing, until the next commit
    or an xpfd restart. RA/Kea withdrawal failures now report the
    rollback DEGRADED instead of claiming the takeover was removed.
  * LLDP: binary Chassis/Port ID subtypes are now decoded canonically
    and keyed by raw MSAP identity (#16673). MAC port IDs (subtype 3)
    and MAC chassis IDs render colon-separated, network-address IDs
    (chassis 5 / port 4) render as the IP literal (or
    iana-<family>:<hex> for non-IP families), printable text stays
    sanitized verbatim, and any other non-printable identifier renders
    hex. Previously such IDs were stored lossy-sanitized with the
    subtype dropped, so distinct NICs under one chassis merged into
    one flapping `show lldp neighbors` entry rendered as garbage, and
    either one's TTL=0 withdrawal removed the other. The neighbor
    table now keys on (interface, chassis subtype + raw bytes, port
    subtype + raw bytes); subtype-truncated binary IDs are rejected
    with the other truncated mandatory TLVs.
  * IPsec: an inline-endpoint VPN whose name equals a gateway object
    referenced by another VPN no longer folds into that gateway's
    swanctl connection (#16676). Strict commit rejects the naming;
    tolerant load renders the inline VPN as `inline-<name>` so each
    peer keeps its own connection, children and st0 if_id. Previously
    the first sorted member supplied the peer and the other VPN's
    traffic was negotiated with the wrong peer with no diagnostic.
  * Daemon/bootstrap: a timed-out first `commit confirmed` now renames
    a non-index-0 foreign-host lifeline back to its pre-takeover kernel
    name and removes its takeover `10-xpf-*.link` (#16675). Bootstrap
    exit captures the (kernel, logical) pair before positional naming
    runs and persists it in the generation-bound lifeline record; the
    first-commit rollback verifies the `.link` `OriginalName=` against
    it, renames back, then removes the file. Previously the rollback
    kept both, leaving the management NIC renamed and unaddressed
    across reboots (its host config keys on the kernel name) while
    reporting success — console-only recovery.
  * Config/cluster: a redundancy-group `node` statement that is not
    exactly `node <0|1> priority <1..254>` is now rejected at commit
    (#16672). A missing `;` fused `node 1 priority 100 node 0
    priority 200` into one statement — the #8437 fused-statement gate
    exempts a same-keyword repeat — and the compiler wrote the second
    priority onto the first node, inverting RG primary placement.
    Missing, non-numeric, duplicate, or trailing priority tokens are
    refused on the same gate; tolerant load warns and keeps the
    first node's own priority.
  * Config/BGP: `multihop` now accepts the Junos spellings (#16679).
    Bare `multihop;` commits at the Junos default TTL 64,
    `multihop { ttl N; }` and flat-set `multihop ttl N` pin the TTL,
    and the legacy `multihop <N>` form stays as an alias; all render
    `neighbor X ebgp-multihop <ttl>`. Previously every Junos spelling
    was rejected at commit. `multihop no-nexthop-change` is refused
    by name until it can be rendered (FRR `ebgp-multihop` rewrites
    the received next hop; the Junos flag retains it).
  * Daemon/commit-confirmed: a nested `commit confirmed` (re-issuing it
    to extend a pending window) now pre-flights the store's retained
    original rollback target for both the appliability and device-map
    gates (#16668). Previously both gates read the unconfirmed active
    config, so extending a window was falsely refused whenever the
    unconfirmed config was not yet appliable (for example it binds a
    dynamic-address feed that has not fetched yet), with an error
    naming a binding the timer would never restore; the true target
    was also never re-checked at the nested arm. The gates now resolve
    the target through `Store.RollbackTargetForArm`, and the refusal
    wording names the rollback target rather than the active config.
  * Feeds: a feed restored with a count baseline but no usable content
    epoch now re-establishes the epoch on its first guarded install
    (#16651). Legacy count-only records, records whose epoch digest was
    dropped as corrupt, and pre-#15075 rotated records restored without
    an epoch previously stayed epoch-less forever, so the #14395
    epoch-anchored churn refusal never engaged and successive
    51%-retained rotations could cumulatively replace an enforced set
    with no refusal. The first install passing every guard (including
    the restored count high-water) now becomes and persists the epoch;
    live epochs are never moved by this arm.
  * Userspace NAT: `release_persistent_inbound` now downgrades the
    `u64::MAX` clear tombstone to `now + 60s` replay horizon when the
    last inbound hold of a revoked persistent lease drops (#16652),
    mirroring `complete_persistent_lease_locked` and `rollback_flow`.
    Previously an inbound-only last holder left the source key
    clear-fenced forever: idle-lease imports were refused
    (`SkippedClearFenced`), synced reserves lost persistence, the
    fence was never pruned, and after failover that host lost its
    persistent mapping (healing only on pool reseed/restart).
  * Dataplane/build: the shim build recipe now runs cargo under
    `env -i` with an explicit allowlist, a repo-controlled empty
    CARGO_HOME, and forced profile/linker `--config` values (#16657),
    and the codegen-env digest is computed from the environment
    actually passed to cargo in the recipe, Go and Python gates.
    Previously only RUSTFLAGS/CARGO_ENCODED_RUSTFLAGS were scrubbed
    and a constant digest recorded, so ambient CARGO_PROFILE_*,
    CARGO_TARGET_* linker/rustflags, RUSTC/RUSTC_WRAPPER,
    CARGO_BUILD_* or $CARGO_HOME config could steer a "pinned" build
    whose verdict both consumer gates accepted as clean; the
    tracked shim object was regenerated under the scrubbed recipe
    (verifier PASS, 696219 insns / 30.38% headroom).
  * Event-options: parked deferred edges are now reconciled on every
    policy Apply (#16650). The pending cooldown/budget maps are keyed
    by policy name (cooldown also by event) with no generation, so a
    predecessor's parked entry previously survived a same-name
    redefine, occupied the key, and silently swallowed the
    redefined policy's in-cooldown edge; the stale entry was then
    deleted at drain on semantic-revision mismatch and the
    successor's only remediation edge (RPM emits ping_test_failed
    only on a status change) was lost. Apply now purges parked
    entries whose stamped revision no longer matches the live
    revision, adjusting the pending-deferred gauge; entries for
    unchanged policies survive and still replay after the cooldown.
  * Daemon/test: `scheduleConnectedRGRepublish`,
    `republishConnectedRGSync` and `scheduleFRRHAReconcile` now
    refuse a nil `applySem` (#16655), like every other background
    semaphore consumer. Previously an HA test fixture built as
    `&Daemon{store, cluster, ...}` without `applySem` panicked in
    a spawned goroutine on demotion/promotion, killing the whole
    `pkg/daemon` test binary after ~609 of 3,495 tests and hiding
    the remaining results (filed as #17474). Production Daemons
    always set `applySem`, so there is no runtime change.
  * Daemon/RPM: `rpmProbeTestGating` source-address→VIP matching is
    now canonical (#16639): RETH unit addresses and `vrrp-group`
    virtual-addresses key the VIP→RG map by parsed `netip.Addr`
    (`Unmap`), as does the test's `source-address` lookup.
    Previously both sides compared the authored strings exactly, so
    a non-canonical IPv6 spelling (case, zero-run) or a 4-in-6 form
    on either side missed, and an ip-monitoring-referenced test
    bound by source-address only fell back to the lowest data RG:
    in a split-RG cluster it ran only on the node that does not
    hold the VIP (held without a verdict) and was dropped on the
    node that does — that uplink's ip-monitoring failover dead
    while ownership was split. Unparseable or unrelated
    source-addresses still match nothing and keep the fallback.
  * Test/denyaudit: the #9042 denial-site census now registers the
    eighth bounded site — the fabric MonitorInterface handler's
    args-bound token mismatch denial (#11082) in
    pkg/grpcapi/server_diag_monitor.go — and pins the count at 8
    (#16648). Previously the census pinned 7, so the package was red
    on correct bounded code and would have gone green if that site's
    bounding were removed, restoring the unbounded per-request Warn
    #9042 eliminated. No production code changed.
  * Packaging/test: `postinst triggered` now resolves the UEFI slot
    helper via `XPF_UEFI_SLOTS_BIN` defaulting to
    `/usr/local/sbin/xpf-uefi-slots` (#16644). Previously the test cell
    `test_postinst_triggered_runs_refresh_and_exits_zero` asserted only
    rc 0 on hosts without the installed helper, so a broken or disabled
    helper invocation in the triggered arm passed undetected; the cell
    now points the override at the in-tree helper and asserts both A/B
    slots are refreshed from the stale fixture, and the no-helper
    control injects a missing path explicitly.
  * Dataplane counters: `ReadInterfaceCounters` no longer returns a
    parent physical interface's whole traffic as a VLAN/logical
    unit's own counters (#16646). Any logical child (ParentIfindex
    distinct from its own ifindex — VLAN-tagged or not, disabled or
    unzoned included) now fails with an explicit unavailable error
    until the helper exports per-(ifindex,vlan) counters, so CLI
    extensive, REST, gRPC and monitor flag the unit unavailable and
    Prometheus omits its samples instead of emitting the parent
    total under every sibling unit label (family sum = real traffic).
    Directly bound interfaces and unit-0 rows collapsed onto the
    parent netdev are unaffected.
  * Event-options: events arriving on a cooldown-parked (policy,event)
    direction are now still windowed (#16641). Previously the parked
    early-continue skipped the sliding-window append, so a
    `within W { trigger until N }` window (N ≳ 4) never reached its
    ceiling during a sustained storm and the remediation replayed
    every 30 s cooldown. Parked events are appended and pruned as
    usual and only their park/fire decision is skipped, so the
    ceiling is reached and the storm stops re-firing; the parked
    crossing itself still replays once at drain, and sparse
    below-ceiling behaviour is unchanged. Parked deferred edges now
    also carry the within-match verdict recorded at park time
    (#16848): the drain replays that verdict under a
    revision/liveness re-check instead of re-deciding it against
    the decayed live window, so a genuine trigger-on crossing with
    a `within` window shorter than the cooldown is no longer
    silently discarded; synthetically parked legacy edges keep the
    #15945 live-window re-check.
  * Daemon/RPM: on the first probe apply after xpfd starts, every
    ip-monitoring-referenced probe now runs the bounded initial
    burst previously used only on HA takeover (#16636). Previously
    a restarted daemon rendered FRR and the helper snapshot from
    an empty ip-monitoring overlay — withdrawing the failover
    preferred route for a dead uplink — and re-derived the FAIL
    verdict only after (successive-loss-1) x test-interval
    (about 120s at defaults), sending traffic back onto the dead
    path for that window on every upgrade or crash-restart.
  * Config/firewall: all four `firewall three-color-policer` burst
    leaves now enforce a one-frame floor of 1514 bytes (#16610):
    `committed-burst-size`/`excess-burst-size` (single-rate) and
    `committed-burst-size`/`peak-burst-size` (two-rate) below 1514
    are rejected at strict commit and warned on tolerant load.
    Previously any positive value (64, 1000, 1k) committed clean;
    because the userspace meter charges the full Ethernet frame,
    such a bucket coloured every maximum-sized frame red at any
    rate and `then discard` dropped it, silently turning the meter
    into a frame-size filter — the three-color twin of the #10310
    single-rate policer floor. A burst at exactly 1514 still
    commits.
  * Routing: rib-group peer-return rules (kernel ip rules in
    1001-1999 and their AF_XDP FIB mirror rows) are now installed
    only for peer prefixes the source instance is authorized to
    reach — its own interface-routes rib-group must name the
    peer's rib (`<peer>.inet.0`/`<peer>.inet6.0`) in import-rib
    (#16616). Previously every tenant leaking its interface
    routes into inet.0 got returns to every other such tenant, so
    two shared-services tenants were reachable from each other in
    VRF context with no import-rib authorizing it. Tenants leaking
    only into main now get `Network is unreachable` for each
    other's prefixes (main->tenant still resolves); add the peer
    rib to import-rib to authorize tenant->tenant returns.
  * Config authz: the #14981 commit-time expanded deny-configuration
    verdict is now bound to the candidate it priced (#16611). The
    gRPC/REST gate stamps the priced candidate generation into the
    commit authority and promotion re-prices a changed candidate
    under the store lock, so a same-holder copy/rename/apply-groups
    landing while a commit waits for the daemon apply lock can no
    longer be promoted unadjudicated; changed candidates must be
    re-committed. Unchanged candidates and unrestricted classes are
    unaffected.
  * Config/NAT: a source-NAT rule whose source match is
    single-family but whose pool has no member of that family
    (an IPv6-only pool, or an IPv4-mapped `::ffff:` literal, for
    an IPv4 rule; or the reverse) is now rejected at strict
    commit (#16600) and warned on tolerant load. Previously it
    committed clean and every matching flow was refused by the
    dataplane allocator as WrongAddressFamily and not forwarded,
    discoverable only through allocation-failure counters — the
    source-NAT twin of the #13922 destination/static cross-family
    reject. Unconstrained and dual-family rules still commit.
  * Config/NAT: the #16600 source-NAT pool family gate is scoped
    to proven mismatches (#17408): it now fires only for a narrow
    single-family source match (a proper subset of its family),
    not a whole-family /0 source match, and the #5627/#5875 pool
    grammar test scaffolds use a dual-family source match so their
    v6-only and unscoped-v6 pool cases exercise pool grammar rather
    than the family gate. A narrow IPv4-only source with a v6-only
    pool (and the reverse) is still rejected at strict commit.
  * PPTP ALG: a first-learn install refused by a permanent alias
    collision is no longer retained as an unbounded self-retry
    (#16609). Previously, on a single-worker helper, a PPTP server
    reusing one call-id alias could grow the retained-install store
    by up to 64 entries/s for the control channel's lifetime, and
    every entry was re-installed and re-counted
    (allocator_collisions +N/s) on the only forwarding worker each
    second. Refusals are now classified: a self-retry is kept only
    while a queued/pending CDN or channel forget will retire the
    colliding association (the transient replacement case), the
    retry walk probes without counting so a retained refusal is
    counted once, and the store is hard-capped (256, drop-newest)
    with an age bound equal to the association idle timeout.
  * DDNS/DHCP: the RFC 4701 device proof marker published under
    skip-existing/strict-fail to let a same-device sibling prove
    co-ownership (#14995) now has an owner and a delete path
    (#16597). Previously it was tracked only in the per-cycle
    updater and never withdrawn, so after a dual-stack lease under
    those policies expired the lone DHCID permanently burned the
    name: the same device returning single-family, or any later
    client, was refused by the name-level guard against xpf's own
    leftover and counted as a third-party conflict. The manager now
    keeps the marker while a same-device sibling survives
    (markerSharedWithSibling/KeepSiblingMarker) and the last
    same-name family's delete withdraws it value-specifically
    alongside the exact A/AAAA delete, so a fully released name
    leaves no RR; deleting a marker that was never published is an
    exact-match no-op and never removes a foreign DHCID.
  * Fragment association: a session at its own 32-entry per-shard quota
    now recycles its OWN oldest association instead of refusing new
    first fragments (#16593). The #11058 refusal capped one NAT'd
    fragmented flow at ~16 datagrams/s (32 entries / 2 s TTL, refreshed
    on every tail hit, never released on completion) and starved
    same-zone sources sharing its shard. Recycling only ever displaces
    the installing session's own entry, so a spoofing session still
    cannot spend or evict another session's quota (#11058), and the
    per-zone (#14321) and full-shard bounds still refuse fail-closed.
    A completed datagram's association is also released when its MF=0
    tail consults, and zone/shard refusals are now counted
    (xpf_userspace_frag_quota_refusals_total); own-quota recycles report
    as live evictions on the existing nat64_frag_assoc_evicted counter.

  * GRE keepalive: a config apply during a peer outage no longer
    re-ups a keepalive-down tunnel (#16582). The dataplane zone
    compile (`mapZoneInterface`) was a second writer of the tunnel's
    admin state: it LinkSetUp'd every zoned netdev moments after
    ApplyTunnels honoured the runner's held-down skipUp, and the
    runner's down edge (`state.Up && Failures>=MaxRetries`) never
    fired again, so the dead tunnel carried routes and FIB ECMP
    selection for the rest of the outage while keepalive status
    still read down. Two defences: the zone loop now consults a
    daemon-registered held-down resolver (routing
    `Manager.TunnelHeldDown`: runner present, Up==false, failures
    at/past threshold) by Linux netdev name before LinkSetUp and
    leaves a held-down tunnel down as an intentionally-down surface;
    and the runner re-downs a link observed admin UP on any dead
    tick at/past threshold (the observed-down steady state issues no
    extra LinkSetDown, and a re-down does not re-fire the
    liveness-change callback). Recovery on the first alive probe is
    unchanged.
  * Config/flow: Junos `security flow tcp-session` spellings
    `tcp-initial-timeout`, `fin-invalidate-session`, and
    `time-wait-state apply-to-half-close-state` now commit (#16581,
    residual of Y39-05/#15256). Previously the closed-world schema
    rejected all three while only the xpf spellings `initial-timeout`
    and `apply-to-half-closed-state` (#15753's misspelling) committed,
    so vSRX flow configs using the Junos leaves were refused at
    commit. The Junos spellings are canonical; the xpf spellings are
    kept as deprecated aliases committing to the same fields
    (`InitialTimeout`, `TimeWaitStateApplyToHalfClosed`), so existing
    committed configs keep working. `fin-invalidate-session` is a new
    enforced knob, not an alias: it compiles to
    `TCPSessionConfig.FinInvalidateSession`, reaches the dataplane as
    `tcp_fin_invalidate_session` (snapshot protocol v71 -> v72, Go and
    Rust in lockstep, so a v70 helper that would ignore it and hold
    FIN-closed sessions for the closing/TIME_WAIT window is fenced by
    exact-version equality), and the Rust close-window selector
    (`tcp_close_window_ns_with_app`) returns an immediate (zero)
    window for the TIME_WAIT class — both FINs seen, close handshake
    complete — while CLOSING (one FIN) and RST keep their windows.
    Strict commit still rejects near-miss keywords, and the
    `tcp-initial-timeout` value keeps the alias's
    `0..MaxDurationSeconds` range gate. `show security flow` renders
    the fin-invalidate row and names the canonical
    `apply-to-half-close-state` in its TIME_WAIT annotation.

  * Userspace event stream: the SessionClose/SessionCreate telemetry queue
    budget is now released when a frame leaves the bounded channel, not when
    an ACK trims it from the replay buffer (#16570). The per-kind budget
    (820 of 8192 channel slots) was held across replay retention until the
    Go daemon's 100 ms ACK tick, so any close/create burst above 820 frames
    in one ACK interval — `clear security flow session`, a mass expiry, a
    policy-revocation sweep, an admission burst — shed queue-full and
    silently lost NetFlow/IPFIX close records (and, since #16303, whole
    flows whose SESSION_CREATE was shed) plus `then log session-close`
    RT_FLOW records, defeating the #14609 never-shed-below-capacity
    contract. Replay retention stays bounded by the 4096-frame replay
    buffer and counted on wrap (#2382, unchanged); the in-channel admission
    bound and the SessionClose 8192/s rate limiter are unchanged, and HA
    session-sync deltas were never budgeted. The #14609 acceptance test now
    drains through the real production drain instead of modelling a
    release the I/O thread never performed.

  * Config: routing-policy `to` match conditions and unmodelled `then`
    action keywords no longer drop silently with only the #10707 advisory
    (#16569). A BGP import term scoped `to neighbor X` (Junos: equivalent
    to `from neighbor X`) previously compiled to a route-filter-only accept
    and was rendered as a permit route-map entry on every peer sharing the
    policy, accepting the prefix from unintended peers; `to protocol` /
    `to level` widened likewise, and a dropped `then preference` kept its
    accept at default preference. Term-level `to` is now recorded on
    PolicyTerm.UnknownTo, statement-level `to` on
    PolicyStatement.UnknownTo, and known Junos `then` actions xpf does not
    model (preference, tag, color, metric2, ...) on PolicyTerm.UnknownThen;
    the #11779 gate strict-rejects all three and tolerant loads warn and
    force affected terms (and the statement default for statement-level
    `to`) to reject, rendering deny. Arbitrary unknown `then` words remain
    the #10707 open-world warn-only case.

  * Daemon/DHCP: an unchanged-lease renewal while host-inbound gap
    debt is owed no longer applies inline on the DHCP client
    goroutine (#16559). The hook parked uncancellably on applySem;
    an apply holding it that stopped/restarted that client then
    waited forever on the client's done channel, wedging every
    later commit, rollback, sync and background apply until
    restart. The hook now nudges the 30s gap retry owner over a
    coalescing wake channel, and the #11497 discharge defer in
    applyConfigLocked is registered before the test seam so the
    retry owner's stubbed applies discharge the debt too.

  * Config: a bare presence statement inline (`set system services
    ssh`, `set system syslog`) no longer discards a group's whole
    container for the same keyword (#16546). The #4325 cross-shape
    guard treated the 1-key leaf as a duplicate of the group's 1-key
    container, so a hardening group's root-login deny, ciphers and
    connection-limit (and group syslog/SNMP/LLDP settings) silently
    never reached the compiled config. A bare `len(Keys)==1` leaf whose
    schema node is a plain container now promotes to an empty container
    and merges the group's children with inline precedence, exactly as
    its empty braced spelling does; valued leaves (packed tails,
    bracketed lists, collapsed leaf-lists) keep the guard, so the
    #4325/#4070 no-duplicate and union invariants are unchanged.

  * NAT: surface the runtime interface-SNAT overlap quarantine on every
    NAT show surface (#16547). The #7717 quarantine stamps
    PoolUnusable/iface_snat_egress_overlap on the applied snapshot when a
    runtime-learned (DHCP/netlink) interface address lands in a source
    pool, refusing every new translation while CLI, gRPC, REST and
    Prometheus — all deriving their verdict from config alone — reported
    the rule armed with a 0 hit counter. The applied snapshot's per-rule
    verdicts (Manager.AppliedNATSnapshotVerdicts, cached in-memory, no
    control-socket I/O) now feed the shared NOT INSTALLED verdict for
    the CLI rule/rule-set/summary/pool/detail and NAT64 views, the gRPC
    GetNATSource/GetNATRuleStats/GetNATPoolStats and ShowText views,
    and the REST source/rule/pool objects (config verdict keeps
    precedence); iface_snat_egress_overlap (and the NAT64 twin, same
    token) expands to operator prose; Prometheus exports
    xpf_iface_snat_overlap_scans_total / _addresses (scans==0 means the
    detector never ran) and xpf_iface_snat_overlap_quarantined{pool},
    and a quarantined pool's capacity/usage samples are omitted like a
    config-refused pool's.
  * Installer: stop creating the developer scratch tree on customer
    hosts (#16539). validate_archive_key pinned its temporary GnuPG
    home under /var/worktrees/.cache/tmpdir whenever TMPDIR was
    unset (always under `curl … | sudo sh`), leaving that tree behind
    and aborting the install when /var could not create it. It now
    uses `mktemp -d -t`, honouring TMPDIR and otherwise falling
    back to /tmp; scripts/dist/install.sh is exempt from the
    #14833 scratch-pinning audit, which covers repo build/test
    scratch only.

  * Routing: default the native-GRE anchor TUN MTU from the underlay
    minus GRE overhead when no MTU is configured (#16517). An
    unconfigured GRE anchor previously kept the kernel TUN default
    1500 (and adoption reset it to 1500), so the local-origin encap
    guard silently dropped every self-originated packet whose outer
    would exceed the transport MTU (inner 1477-1500 IPv4-outer,
    1457-1500 IPv6-outer) with no Frag-Needed/PTB back into the TUN.
    Created, adopted, and stale owned GRE anchors now carry
    1500-overhead (1476/1472 IPv4 unkeyed/keyed, 1456/1452 IPv6),
    mirroring native_gre_inner_mtu; a configured MTU is unchanged
    and a smaller hand-set MTU is never raised.
  * wire-routing-separation #11416 fixture-shape guards: stop matching
    the script's own text (#16516). The two cells matched $(cat "$0")
    against needles that also appear on the guard's own lines, so
    dropping the populated lan->wire-vrf policy and SNAT statements
    (and their cleanup deletes) stayed green. The populated add/delete
    transaction is now rendered by a single pure printer,
    populated_cli_transcript, which cli_commit_populated pipes live and
    the selftest calls directly with the fixture defaults pinned,
    asserting the rendered zone/policy/SNAT sets and cleanup deletes
    as whole lines (and no wan zone). The policy/NAT-drop mutant now
    reds both cells and a wan-zone mutant still reds the first
    (33 cells, unchanged count).
  * Userspace DP: generated-reply limiter keys IPv6 per-/128 inside a
    bounded /64 again (#16510). #15248 keyed solely on the /64, so every
    host of one LAN /64 shared a single 15/s + burst-15 budget and one
    host at ~15 pps suppressed its neighbours' Time Exceeded / Packet
    Too Big / reject replies. Hosts now keep independent fair-share
    budgets (15/s) with the /64 as an outer tier at 32 host shares
    (480/s), so IID rotation stays bounded, one /64 cannot take more
    than ~48% of the zone aggregate, and 30 same-/64 hosts burst 90/90.
    Denial still fails closed; the 1000/s zone cap is unchanged.

  * Daemon-kernel probe selftest: kill the -json=false, SKIP-guard
    and go-exit mutants (#16507). Cell 9 asserted only the leg PASS
    line, and its fake go's argv line was discarded by the leg's
    output capture on a passing run, so dropping -json=false from
    the leg survived. The argv line now goes to a side file and is
    asserted to carry <-json=false>. Two new cells pin the
    remaining guards: a top-level SKIP on a legacy cell must fail
    by name, and a fake go that prints the full PASS list but
    exits 1 must fail with 'go test exited 1'. Each of the three
    mutants now reds the probe selftest (15 cells, was 13).

  * Userspace DP: deliver D11 terminals for stale-generation survivor
    descriptors and for every stop_inner reap (#16497, #15149 residual).
    Retiring a dead worker bumps the global worker-set generation, so
    descriptors queued on surviving workers read stale at their next
    drain and were dropped with no verdict; stop_inner likewise reaped
    every queue into an undelivered verdict list. The drain stale branch
    now records a d11_stale_generation Deny when its tombstone claim
    wins, the worker loop delivers reaped terminals on the same pass,
    and stop_inner drains them into the completion core after joining
    its workers. Affected requests now complete as Denied immediately
    instead of by the 5 ms Go ack deadline; enforcement is unchanged
    (fail-closed either way).

  * Screen: cap per-source scan/sweep state at the detection count
    (#16498). Port-scan/IP-sweep detection fires at 10 distinct
    destinations per source window, but each source kept inserting
    into a heap set up to 1024 entries, the window reset retained
    that allocation, and eviction preserved the fullest sets — a
    spoofed-source fan-out flood could pin ~144 MiB (ip-sweep)
    plus ~24 MiB (port-scan) per worker per screened zone.
    Recorded destinations now live in an inline 10-slot buffer in
    each source record (no per-source heap), the recorded-entry cap
    equals the detection count, and distinct destinations past the
    count count on the existing skipped-pressure signal with the
    verdict unchanged. Saturated per-source records are 200 B
    (ip-sweep) / 64 B (port-scan), i.e. at most ~1.03 MiB per
    worker per zone for both screens' source records.

  * Userspace DP: stop cloning counter/policer handles on live
    TX-selection walks (#16496). `merge_matched_tx_modifiers` pushed
    an `Arc::clone` of every matched term's counter and three-color
    policer into the result on every walk, including the metered and
    counted live transit walks that immediately discard them — a
    strong-count RMW on the cross-worker shared line per counted
    term per packet, plus a heap spill at three or more handles.
    Handles are now collected only by the deferred generated-reply
    walk (#15268), via a threaded `collect_deferred` flag and the
    new `evaluate_filter_ref_tx_selection_deferred` entry point;
    live walks charge inline exactly as before and collect nothing,
    and the deferred replay still charges exactly once after
    admission. No enforcement or accounting behaviour changes.

  * Docs: correct the session module contract for #9412 and the
    established timeout (#16493). The session README still documented
    #9412 as OPEN — close state "does NOT cross" the HA session-sync
    wire, imported sessions ageing on a 300 s established window, and a
    two-sided guard cell that "FAILS when #9412 is fixed" — although
    #9412 closed with close state riding
    `SessionSyncRequest.tcp_close_class` and the established default
    has been 1800 s since #15127. The README now describes the shipped
    carrier and import semantics, matches the guard cell's current
    header, and uses 1800 s for the present-tense established figures;
    a new pkg/docsref gate pins the contract (including the #16486
    OSPF 60 s / Other-protocols 1800 s rows). Documentation only; no
    runtime behaviour changes.
  * Build/test: shorten the pinned GOTMPDIR root to /wtgt (#16494).
    The #14833 pin used /var/worktrees/.cache/go-tmp (28 chars); Go
    test TempDir paths under it exceed the 107-octet AF_UNIX
    sun_path limit (120 octets for a worst-case control.sock), so
    45+ socket tests failed with "bind: invalid argument" under
    `make test-go`. GOTMPDIR (Makefile, run-selftests.sh, and the
    memlock-guards leg) now defaults to /wtgt (5 chars, 97-octet
    worst case), and scratch-pinning-selftest replaces its modelled
    71-byte path check with the executed scripts/sunpath-canary
    bind, required to pass under /wtgt and fail under a long root.
  * Userspace DP: stop refusing admissible D11 IPsec-inner frames on
    lock contention (#16495). Router in-flight, worker-set authority
    and tombstone admission took try_lock and refused as
    WORKER_QUEUE_FULL / EVALUATOR_UNAVAILABLE / LEASE_EPOCH (mapped to
    ADMIT_FULL / ADMIT_SHUTDOWN / ADMIT_BAD_LEASE drops and a false
    attestation FAIL); they now take blocking locks with bounded holds,
    so E23 moves only at genuine queue/per-flow capacity.

  * Userspace DP: make deferred conntrack-delete retries O(1) and
    poison-recovering (#16491). The housekeeping retry locked the
    shared-session map with .expect (re-panicking on a poisoned
    mutex where every other site recovers) and scanned every shared
    value per retry, up to 64 O(N) scans per pass under the
    cross-worker mutex. It now uses the maintained bare-tuple index
    plus an exact-scope lookup under lock_shared_recover, mirroring
    close_delta_is_stale_incarnation; a shared row in any scope
    sharing the bare tuple still fences the delete (the kernel
    conntrack row is bare-tuple keyed).


  * Configstore: retire a #15273-deferred api-auth migration staging
    record when a durable promotion supersedes it (#16492). The
    deferred boot left `.configdb/api-auth-migration.json` and the
    pre-migration rollback-sidecar binding in place; the next plain
    commit (the documented remedy for the /health-503 lost-window
    alarm) or HA config sync wrote a new active generation and rebound
    the sidecar, so the following boot's staging-consistency gate
    matched neither staged generation and xpfd refused to start
    (ErrConfigDBUnreadable) on every boot until an operator deleted a
    file no message named. Plain commit, commit-confirmed, SyncApply,
    rollback, boot-recovery, and persist-retry heal now retire staging
    once the sidecar binds the new disk generation exactly, Load
    re-checks supersession before the gate so a lost retirement
    self-heals, and the fatal error names the staging file. Genuinely
    mismatched generations stay fail-closed.


  * API/daemon: surface journal legacy-scrub degradation on /health
    and Prometheus (#16490). The #15143 `JournalLegacyScrubDegraded`
    flag latched when a pre-#1896 journal segment could not be
    rewritten or purged (cleartext api-auth credentials may survive),
    but unlike its `JournalPermsDegraded` twin it had no consumer
    outside configstore. The daemon now wires
    `JournalLegacyScrubDegradedFn` beside `JournalPermsDegradedFn`:
    a non-fatal `journal_legacy_scrub_degraded` /health field (stays
    200, appends continue) plus the
    `xpf_config_journal_legacy_scrub_degraded` gauge, emitted even
    when the dataplane is not loaded, clearing after a successful
    scrub retry.
  * Userspace DP: default other-protocol sessions to 1800 s and OSPF
    to 60 s (#16486). GRE/ESP/AH/SCTP/6in4 and every other protocol
    without a TCP/UDP/ICMP arm idled out after 30 s, so on one-way
    policies the first remote packet after a lull was policy-denied
    (e.g. inbound ESP on an idle IPsec passthrough tunnel). The Junos
    protocol table gives "Other" 30 minutes and OSPF 1 minute; the
    single session-timeout resolver and the show-path now use those
    values, while a promoted session's application
    inactivity-timeout still wins.
  * Userspace DP: include round-robin NAT64 BIB leases in the
    persistent-NAT show table, clear verb, and HA idle export
    (#16488). Every round-robin NAT64 flow mints a persistent
    AnyRemoteHost BIB lease (#13946), but the coordinator's idle
    export, display export, and clear loops gated on
    `deterministic_v6` (#14676/#14091 covered only deterministic
    NAT64), so round-robin BIBs were invisible to
    `show security nat source persistent-nat-table`, survived
    `clear security nat source persistent-nat-table`, and never
    reached the HA peer while idle. The three loops now cover every
    NAT64 prefix (round-robin under `nat64-{idx}`), and imports of
    `nat64-{idx}` records validate pool membership, the allocator's
    live port range, and peer/static/interface ownership with the
    clear fence before installing.
  * Userspace DP: seed the CLOSING counter baseline at the closing
    transition (#16489). The #14937 expiry retention compared the
    forward packet total against a `closing_counters_seen` field
    initialised to zero, so pre-FIN traffic and the FIN packet itself
    read as post-close activity on the first expiry check and every
    silent half-closed pair was retained one extra close window
    (~60 s by default, a full extra service timeout under
    apply-to-half-closed session-ageout). The baseline is now seeded
    when a pair first becomes CLOSING (session-hit, deferred-owner,
    and HA-promote stamps) and from the carried wire total for a
    synced import that arrives CLOSING; genuine post-close traffic
    still retains, and TIME_WAIT/RST keep their fixed fuse.
  * Config: render the enforced close windows under time-wait-state
    (#16485). `RenderFlowTimeouts` (the CLI/gRPC/REST SSOT) printed
    `closing-timeout` / `time-wait-timeout` even when
    `time-wait-state session-ageout` held closed sessions for the
    established/service window (1800s by default) or
    `apply-to-half-closed-state` governed CLOSING sessions with the
    TIME_WAIT window. Both rows now derive from the dataplane's
    close-window selector with "service/established timeout
    (session-ageout)" / "time-wait window (apply-to-half-closed-state)"
    annotations; `session-ageout` together with `session-timeout` is
    rejected at strict commit and warned on tolerant loads, where
    ageout wins and show reports it.
  * Userspace DP: count a Stage-11 IPsec pre-claim PBR drop exactly once
    (#16480). The #14314 pre-claim probe resolved the input-filter PBR
    override uncounted and recycled on `RouteOverride::Drop`, but that
    arm is terminal — the packet never reaches the claimed arm or miss
    path where the counted evaluation runs — so an IKE packet dropped
    by a `routing-instance X; reject|discard` term moved no term
    counter, emitted no filter log, got no reject reply, and an
    Unresolvable drop on a quarantined domain missed the #11061
    ambiguous-fabric count. The Drop arm now re-runs the counted
    evaluation once with a reject sink plus the #11061 count before
    recycling; `Table`/`None` stay uncounted so steered/claimed packets
    still count exactly once.
  * NAT64: select the previous prefix by rule identity on reload
    (#16484). The #6765 retained-address reseed, the deterministic
    probe, and allocator reuse keyed on the first previous prefix
    with equal /96 bytes, so since #15678 a pool edit on any scoped
    same-/96 rule-set after the first reseeded from a sibling's
    allocator (carrying nothing, or suppressed by a deterministic
    first rule) and the fresh allocator reissued live tuples to
    other clients of that rule-set. Reload lookups now match on
    the rule-set name, falling back to prefix bytes plus the
    normalized zone/routing-instance/source scope for nameless
    legacy states.
  * Config: declare `family inet6 dhcpv6` in the schema (#16476). The
    bare shorthand was consumed by the compiler (unit.DHCPv6) and the
    daemon started an AFInet6 DHCPv6 client for it, but setSchema
    declared only `dhcpv6-client`, so the #10707 open-world advisory
    falsely warned that `dhcpv6` "may be silently ignored" on every
    commit and `set ... family inet6 dhcpv6` had no completion or `?`
    help. The bare flag is now a declared leaf beside `dhcpv6-client`;
    `dhcpv6x` still draws the advisory and compiles to no client.
  * Test: move standalone and legacy-local lab NAT pools off shared-
    segment addresses owned by other stacks (#16473). On the shared
    172.16.50.0/24 segment the standalone VM's NAT64 pool sat on
    172.16.50.11 (the udp cluster's WAN RETH) and its SNAT pool /
    proxy-ARP on 172.16.50.6 (the legacy local cluster's WAN RETH);
    co-running stacks would ARP-fight and mis-deliver NAT replies.
    The standalone pools move to .14/.15 (both proxy-ARPed, so the
    NAT64 pool gains the ARP answerer it never had), the legacy
    local fw0 view's SNAT pool moves off .7 (the loss legacy RETH)
    to .12 with its NAT64 pool .9 proxy-ARPed, the standalone and
    legacy-local stacks join the docs/incus-tcp-udp-plan.md §3.3
    registry, and a hermetic configstore census compiles every
