  * Userspace-dp: replace the dead #812 "Rc not Arc" cell with a real
    !Send/!Sync proof (#16447). The old cell coerced three WorkerUmem
    methods to fn pointers whose signatures never mention Rc and made
    no assertion, so an Rc->Arc migration of WorkerUmem.inner compiled
    and passed while its comment claimed a runtime two-clone assertion
    that did not exist. The replacement pins WorkerUmem as neither
    Send nor Sync with module-level ambiguous-trait compile-time
    proofs (making WorkerUmem Send or Sync fails to compile the test
    binary) and actually asserts clone allocation-sharing via the
    hermetic new_for_test constructor. Test only; WorkerUmem stays Rc.
  * Userspace-dp: anchor the #15244 post-create-bind tripwire per arm
    (#16437). The fill-prime and non-zerocopy arms of try_open_bind must
    each carry `return Err(post_create_bind_error(...))` inside their
    own windows in try_open_bind's comment-stripped body; the previous
    file-wide count floor was met by the tag definition, the `is_`
    predicate and the ladder gate alone, so un-tagging either inline
    arm kept the tripwire green. Mutants un-tagging or comment-rescuing
    either arm now red the tripwire; the baseline stays green.
  * Userspace-dp: make L3 reverse bucket protocol counts sparse (#16435).
    #15087's L3ReverseBucket embedded a [u32; 256] count array in every
    NAT (server, translated-address) identity, costing 1104 B per index
    slot (plus a 1 KiB zero-fill per new identity) versus ~60 B pre-fix —
    about 310 MB extra per worker at the 131072-session cap with
    distinct identities. The counts are now a SmallVec<[(u8, u32); 2]>
    holding one pair per live protocol (zero-count pairs are dropped),
    so the (key, bucket) slot is back under 128 B with no heap allocation
    for the common 1-3-protocol bucket. Hashed membership, exact and
    255-sentinel probe gating, and the bounded verification walk are
    unchanged; pinned by the new size/sparsity cells in
    l3_reverse_bucket_15087_tests.rs alongside the existing #15087 cells.
  * Userspace helper: ignore SIGHUP in xpf-userspace-dp (#16431).
    #15084 made xpfd ignore SIGHUP, but the Rust helper kept the
    default disposition (xpfd's signal.Notify capture resets to
    SIG_DFL across exec), so `systemctl kill -s HUP xpfd` still
    killed the forwarding helper and transit failed closed until
    the supervised respawn. The helper now installs SIG_IGN for
    SIGHUP at the top of run(), before workers spawn; SIGINT and
    the shutdown verb keep their graceful-stop semantics. Pinned
    by userspace-dp/tests/sighup_16431.rs (real binary survives a
    real SIGHUP and keeps answering ping).
  * Dataplane: a port-mirroring input in Linux spelling (`ge-0-0-1`) on
    a port with an untagged unit 0 no longer installs as a unit-0
    VID-0-only row (#16429). The untagged unit-0 row shares the base
    netdev name, and its unit scope overwrote the base row's scope
    under the shared Linux key, so tagged sibling units were silently
    not mirrored while show reported the instance installed. A base
    row's LinuxName key now wins over later unit rows on the same
    netdev; the Linux unit spelling (`ge-0-0-1.0`) still resolves
    unit-scoped, and the #15113/#15651/#16063 mirror cells stay green.

  * Userspace-dp: fix #16444 fixture `pkt_len` drift and harden its guard.
    The #6883 contract (shim stamps the FULL frame length, #8581) was
    violated by `resolver_enqueue_tests.rs` (the master-red site), the
    shared `txn_meta_v6` helper and 50+ sibling fixtures spelling the
    subtraction as `(frame_len - 14)`, `frame.len() as u16 - 14`, `- l3`,
    `(inner.len() - 14)` or via `txn_meta_v4(.., (len - 14) as u16)` —
    all now stamp the full buffer length, so CoS/policer/flow-cache byte
    paths are exercised at production lengths (byte-counter assertions
    read `meta.pkt_len`, so they follow automatically). GRE fixture
    helpers `gre_to_self_outer_meta{,_v6}` were likewise stamping
    `frame_len - l3`. The guard no longer matches three literals: it
    flags any comment-stripped `pkt_len` value or `txn_meta_v4` third
    argument containing a header subtraction, while non-`pkt_len`
    wire-length invariants (IPv4 total / IPv6 payload length) stay
    legal; its stale "nothing gates on pkt_len" header is corrected.
    Red on each reintroduced spelling, green on the fixed tree.

  * CLI: show security flow session renders stored session provenance
    (#16110). Each row now carries Origin (local / cluster-synced, from
    the SessFlagClusterSynced bit; promotion clears it so a promoted
    row reads local) and Forwarding-ready (local forward packets), so a
    standby copy is no longer indistinguishable from a locally
    forwarding owner row. Address-only NAT (SNAT/DNAT flag set, stored
    port 0) renders the preserved original port instead of a
    synthesized /0 on CLI, REST and gRPC (the userspace conntrack
    mirror now also stores the effective preserved source port), and
    when the local and peer views of the same synced tuple carry
    different translated ports the CLI prints an explicit NAT
    divergence line naming both ports. Peer rows report the peer's own
    Origin over a new SessionEntry.origin wire field (unknown from a
    pre-change peer).

xpf (0.0.0) unstable; urgency=medium

  * NAT: apply the persistent-NAT lease-batch peer cap (16 distinct
    origins) to generation-0 first contact as well (#16417). The
    #14963 cap was guarded by Generation > 0, but the gen-0 arm still
    persisted a new RemoteGenerations key, so a misbehaving HA sync
    peer emitting gen-0 batches from 17+ origins wrote a generation
    state file the loader rejects on every restart. Gen-0 first
    contact keeps its no-clear-barrier semantics within the cap.
  * Dataplane: warn-and-skip the six tolerated NAT/policy shapes on
    the production real pass as well as the #4960 validation pre-pass
    (#16422). A leniently loaded or HA-synced config carrying a
    dangling SNAT/DNAT pool, a network-prefix DNAT pool, a
    mixed-family static NAT rule, a bad NAT64 prefix or unusable
    NAT64 source pool, or a policy naming an undefined zone no
    longer aborts the apply after Phase 2 has mutated the host and
    publishes no snapshot; the offending rule/rule-set/zone-pair is
    skipped fail-closed exactly as the userspace snapshot builders
    already disarm it, and strict commit rejection in pkg/config is
    unchanged. compileNPTv6 now only examines IsNPTv6 rules, so a
    plain static NAT rule is no longer rejected by the NPTv6 prefix
    grammar.
  * FRR: restore per-qualified-next-hop metrics for routes redistributed
    into RIP (#12531). RIP now receives its QNH metric through
    ripd-native offset-lists in the router rip stanza (one
    offset-list per QNH destination, offset = metric-1 clamped so the
    advertised metric never exceeds 16, with a generated access-list
    permitting exactly that destination) instead of the default metric.
    This supersedes the #12071 RIP limitation: FRR 10.7.1 daemon-scoped
    ripd loads silently apply nothing, integrated route-map loads
    broadcast the metric to other daemons, and ripd ignores route-map
    set metric for redistributed statics even when present — all
    live-verified, with the RIPv2 wire now carrying the configured
    metric (e.g. 10) while OSPF behavior is unchanged.
  * Userspace-dp: split afxdp/types/forwarding.rs (2,297 lines) into
    forwarding/{mod,state,fib,local,tunnels,zones,fabric,disposition,
    binding}.rs (#16209). File-only: definitions move behind the existing
    `pub(in crate::afxdp) use forwarding::*` glob; zero access-site,
    layout, or behaviour change, and no visibility widening (each sibling
    shifts `use super::*` to `use super::super::*`, one in-body super path
    gains a level). ForwardingState's 128-field definition stays intact in
    state.rs — this does not discharge the accepted-debt obligation to
    split that definition itself.
  * Test: count whole-run per-stream iperf3 zero intervals only outside
    each [T, T+3] transition window at the pinned budget 0 (#12618).
    `scripts/iperf-json-metrics.py` gains repeatable `--event-sec` plus
    `--event-window-sec 3` and emits
    `stream_zero_intervals_outside_events_total` /
    `zero_streams_outside_events_total`: only a full-length (>=0.95s)
    per-stream interval wholly inside a window is excluded, so partial
    or straddling intervals still count (fail-closed). The raw
    `stream_zero_intervals_total` is retained for diagnostics.
    `ha_metrics_gate` requires the new counters; `ha-smoke-iperf-lib.sh`
    passes both recorded transition seconds and gates on the outside
    count under `HA_SMOKE_MAX_STREAM_ZERO_INTERVALS` (named in the FAIL
    cell), and `test-failover.sh` does the same for its crash/failback
    seconds under `MAX_STREAM_ZERO_INTERVALS`. Hermetic pins in
    `iperf-throughput-selftest.sh` cover in-window exclusion, straddle
    and partial counting, the late-stall failure, and both budget-0
    defaults; `ha-assurance-selftest.sh` pins gate malformed/absent
    handling for the new fields. The 3s width is the issue's preferred
    calibration hypothesis (lab silent-death detection ~1.0-2.2s plus
    the strict 3s stream-resume oracle); healthy-lab runs of
    test-double-failover, test-chained-crash and test-failover remain
    the operator-gated acceptance for the width.
  * Lab: make the WAN target 172.16.80.200 declarative (#16213). It was
    a hand `ip addr add` alias on xpf-mouse-target beside the pinned
    .201 — any recreate/reboot silently dropped it while harnesses kept
    measuring .200. TARGET_ALIAS_V4 (derived by cluster-env.sh from
    IPERF_TARGET4: loss .200, tcp .210, udp .211) is now pinned by
    mouse-addr.service in mouse-target-setup.sh when it differs on
