    so the covered baseline is shellcheck-clean.

xpf (0.0.0) unstable; urgency=medium
  * Harness: wire-gate captures are detached in-container with a
    watchdog bound (#16329). A backgrounded `incus exec ... timeout
    N tcpdump` orphaned tcpdump to PID 1 (unkillable from later exec
    sessions) when the local client was killed — tcp runs hung
    1263s/279s against a 120s STEP_TIMEOUT with no verdict.
    wire-gate-lib.sh now starts each capture via one foreground exec
    (`nohup tcpdump -w <pcap> & echo $! >pidfile` plus a detached
    `( sleep <lifetime>; kill -TERM <pid> )` watchdog; TERM because
    backgrounded processes inherit SIGINT-ignored), stops it by the
    recorded pid from a fresh exec, VERIFIES via pgrep on the unique
    savefile that nothing survives (loud VOID, never a discarded
    pkill), and pulls/renders the pcap locally so all text reductions
    are unchanged. Converted: wire-policy-deny, wire-zone-matrix,
    wire-appmatch-twins, wire-hostinbound-deny,
    wire-conntrack-lifecycle, wire-nat-frag-needed,
    wire-routing-separation and test-fbf-steering's peer capture,
    each with a stable remote pidfile base that is precleaned before
    a new window. Hermetic stub selftests prove: stop works after
    the starter exec is gone, an unkillable capture fails loud, and
    with no stop at all the watchdog ends the capture (verified live
    on a scratch container: SIGKILLed harness, `pgrep tcpdump`
    empty within the lifetime).
  * Userspace-DP: an HA watchdog set containing an activation-pending
    RG (stored inactive, incoming active) now routes the whole set to
    the locked path instead of storing it from the session fast path
    (#16382). Previously a mixed set still stored with sibling
    refreshes counted, consuming the activation without the locked
    path's epoch bump, RefreshOwnerRGS, prewarm or neighbor warm, and
    a later tick could mint the lease from the match arm — at an RG
    failover instant the fabric-adoption gate then read a stale
    inactive snapshot and declined ha_inactive for seconds.
    Activations are published only by Coordinator::update_ha_state;
    demotion arms (valid-lease mint, expired never resurrected) and
    steady-state matching sets are unchanged.
  * Build: add a hermetic proto header check with CI-only regeneration
    (#16195). `make proto-check` compares the protoc / protoc-gen-go /
    protoc-gen-go-grpc stamps in the checked-in headers against the
    pinned constants recorded in proto/README.md and the protobuf
    version in go.mod (file reads only, no network/protoc); a second,
    CI-only `make proto-regen-check` regenerates with the pinned
    protoc 3.21.12 release zip and pinned plugins and fails on any
    diff, catching a .proto edited without regeneration independently
    of the header red. Local regeneration behaviour is unchanged.

  * Parameterize mouse/DMZ target instance names per cluster env
    (#16328): tcp declares MOUSE_TARGET_NAME=xpf-tcp-target and udp
    declares xpf-udp-target (loss keeps xpf-mouse-target
    byte-identical); cluster-env.sh derives remote-qualified
    MOUSE_TARGET_REF/DMZ_TARGET_REF and exports INCUS_REMOTE.
    Wire gates (policy-deny, appmatch-twins, zone-matrix,
    hostinbound-deny, nat-frag-needed, conntrack-lifecycle,
    routing-separation), test-host-inbound, test-fbf-steering and
    cc-rollback resolve their VLAN-80/50 probers through those refs
    instead of hardcoded loss names. wire-zone-matrix passes
    INCUS_REMOTE explicitly to mouse-target-setup.sh and preserves
    its output on DMZ-prober setup failure; mouse-target-setup
    fails `up` when the iperf3/python3/tcpdump install fails.
    cluster-env-selftest asserts the real per-cluster names.

  * Build: script tests now load their subjects through one test-only
    helper, `load_subject` in scripts/lib (#16194, Stage A). The
    repeated `spec_from_file_location` scaffolding is gone from all
    83 script and test/incus test files; the helper registers the
    module in `sys.modules` before executing it (preserving the
    dataclass and identity semantics the hand-registered tests relied
    on), removes that registration and restores any prior entry when
    execution fails, and raises on a missing file. Production CLI
    files keep their hyphenated names and invocation paths; no
    packages, shims, or Stage-B layout change.
  * Build: add a systemd-unit canary over the explicit staging lists
    (#16198). A new python self-test reads debian/rules as joined make
    lines, bake.py through its AST string constants, and the unit
    sources as systemd directives, then checks the staged/enabled/
    cleaned .deb sets and the bake --copy-in/enable sets against a
    test-data-only manifest. The intentional image/deb divergence is
    carried by explicit allowlists (image-only xpf-grow-root and
    incus-agent; deb-only xpfd, via its sed verify-dataplane transform,
    and xpf-day0-config), every OnFailure=/RequiredBy= target must be
    staged or an allowlisted external unit, and seeded dropped-unit /
    dangling-reference counterfactuals prove the comparator reds.
    No generator: the explicit lists stay the source of truth.
  * API: metrics descriptor init and Describe are now driven by one
    per-family descriptor table (#16202). The ~36 init calls in
    newCollector, the 630-line Describe enumeration, and the seven
    per-family describe helpers are replaced by a statically allocated
    37-row table binding each family's init to the descriptor fields
    it assigns; Describe allocates nothing per call and a new
    agreement test fails if a family's init and its table row drift.
    Metric names, help text, and the described set are unchanged
    (648 descriptors, byte-identical), and Collect is untouched.

  * FRR: extract the `danglingRefs` helper in the policy term renderer
    (#16207). The as-path (#9881), community (#10822) and prefix-list
    (#14136) fail-closed term rules each open-coded the same
    skip-empty / probe-each-distinct-name-once / any-dangling
    collection over a `map[string]bool`; they now share the one
    helper, which allocates the same single map per site and adds no
    pass. Naming, goto targets, sequence numbering and emitted FRR
    output are byte-for-byte unchanged (hash unification of generated
    names remains rejected — those names are a stability contract).
  * FRR: the per-instance protocol renderer now skips forwarding
    instances (`VRFName=="" && TableID>0`) as a defensive backstop
    (#16204). Such an instance has no VRF device, so carrying BGP or
    another dynamic protocol previously rendered a second, unscoped
    `router bgp` stanza joining the global AS. Normal VRF instances
    and the legacy empty-VRF/no-table shape are unaffected.
  * Routing: the rib-name matcher and rib-group leak cap are now defined
    once in pkg/config (`RibInstanceFromName`, `MaxRibGroupLeakRules`,
    #16203). pkg/routing imports both instead of keeping verbatim copies,
    so the commit-time gate and the runtime applier cannot disagree on
    what resolves or fits. The unverified third resolver in
    pkg/routing/routes.go is unchanged.
  * Cmdtree: `LookupDesc` is now the description projection of the
    single completion walk instead of a third hand-rolled walker
    (#16151), and a per-keyword agreement test pins that every
    keyword completion offers is a keyword `Canonicalize` accepts
    (full spelling and unique abbreviation, descriptions agreeing),
    with counting-provider and AST pins that no provider is
    reachable from `Canonicalize`. The rejected step-3 merge of
    `Canonicalize` into a provider-capable walk stays rejected.
    Repeated option keywords now describe nothing in `?` help,
    mirroring the canonicalizer's refusal of the finished line;
    the offering surface is unchanged.
  * Config: `compactNormalizeInScope` now has a reachability audit
    (#16150). All 646 case pairs plus the non-switch
    `policy proposal-set` admission are classified: exactly two
    (`authentication md5`, `authentication simple-password`) are
    shadowed by the `authentication`/`user` pre-emption and can
    never fire through the predicate — their OSPF spellings are
    handled by the packed-body readers — and the other 645 are
    each observed firing through the production fold at a real
    schema position. The guard comment that claimed no pair named
    a pre-empted container is corrected; no admission changed.
  * Dataplane: the management surfaces (REST, CLI, gRPC) now share
    the optional-capability helpers in `pkg/dataplane/mgmtview`
    (`Probe`/`Unwrap`, `MapStats`, `SessionCount`,
    `SessionCountContext`) instead of triplicated `dpProbe` and
    per-surface `backendMapStats`/`backendSessionCount` copies
    (#16155). No combined `Full` interface is introduced: the three
    mandatory surfaces stay separate, and optional caps are still
    resolved fresh from the backend published at each answer —
    exactly one publication per answer, never cached across backend
    replacement — so operator output is unchanged.

  * Split pkg/config/schema_walk.go (2,369 lines) mechanically into
    the schema_walk_* family: schema_walk_entry.go (SchemaValidate*
    entry point and the exit secret-scrub), schema_walk_refs.go
    (walkContext and collectSchemaRefs), schema_walk_leaf.go (the
    typed-leaf validators), and schema_walk_errors.go (the typed /
    unknown-stanza error types, every error constructor, and the
    #16066 secret-scrubbing choke point), leaving the walk itself
    (walkSchemaChildren/walkSchemaNode/walkInstanceChildren) in
    schema_walk.go. Same package, no statement, signature or
    behaviour change and no test file modified; imports pruned per
    file (#16152). The deferred PR2 nodeKind classifier is not added.
  * Config: `BodyView` (Children/Find over the shared packed-body
    expansion, with a per-family expansion hook) is adopted by the
    screen family first (#16148). compileScreen's family lookups, the
    ids-option packed-body read, family normalization and the
    duplicate-family check now go through the view; the bespoke
    `screenFamilyBodyChain` expander is deleted (its bracketed chain
    is inlined into the one remaining screen walk). The plain
    (nested) branch stays zero-allocation, pinned by an alloc gate,
    and old-vs-new expander output is pinned node-for-node on
    hierarchical/flat/packed/bracketed forms. No behaviour change:
    the screen family's unknown-token backstop and expansion order
    are preserved exactly, which is the per-family falsifying
    experiment the campaign's stop-rule requires.

  * Config: the three strict consumed/identity token walks
    (ResolveConsumedSetPathTokens, editIdentityStarts,
    editIdentityConsumed) now share one token-walk kernel,
    walkStrictConsumedTokens (#16149), replacing three independently
    re-derived exact/unique-prefix/wildcard/compound-key walks. The
    only deliberate divergence is strictness (requireUniquePrefix:
    ResolveConsumed rejects ambiguous/unknown consumed tokens, the
    edit-identity walk merely stops segmentation), pinned by a
    published divergence table and an agreement test with seeded
    mutants. ResolveConsumed now also prefix-expands a compoundKey
    sub-token (previously appended verbatim). CompleteSetPathWithValues
    (partial completion) is deliberately untouched pending its
    divergence census.
  * Dataplane: the three string protocol carriers (filter, policy
    application, DNAT) now have per-carrier validation cells proving
    atomic `__unsupported__` poison preservation, both mixed-version
    refusal orders, and protocol-0 (HOPOPT) semantics before any future
    u8 retype (#16134). The Rust protocol resolvers also reject
    non-canonical numerics such as `+6` that the Go SSOT refuses, and
    the policy resolver shares the single strict resolver with the
    filter/DNAT paths.

  * Config: the three config-commit surfaces now share exactly three
    config-lifecycle helpers in pkg/configstore (#16156):
    `Store.ConfirmOnly()` (the confirm-pending && candidate-unchanged
    predicate, read under one store lock hold instead of two separate
    flag reads), `ClassifyCommitError` (the structural (compiled, err)
    commit-error classification shared by the REST and gRPC commit
    handlers), and `WarningsOf` (nil-safe defensive-copy extraction of
    `cfg.Warnings`). There is no configops.Service and no
    authority-minting CommitGate: REST/gRPC still mint holder-epoch
    authority before the confirm gate and the local CLI still commits
    authority-free, so operator-visible behaviour is unchanged.

  * Config: the interface resolvers (`ResolveReth`, `ResolveFab`,
    `DHCPLeaseKey`) now share one extracted first-dot parser,
    `SplitIfaceRef`, which returns the suffix RAW and uninterpreted
    (#16144). Suffix-copying callers keep arbitrary suffix text
    verbatim (including non-decimal and multi-dot forms) and
    `DHCPLeaseKey` keeps discarding the suffix in favour of its
    separately supplied unit number; a universal integer-unit parser
    was rejected as unsound for these callers. Declared dotted
    interface names (e.g. an authored `gr-0/0/0.0`) continue to
    resolve through the declared-name-wins resolver arm unchanged.

  * NAT: NATMatch consumers now read match criteria through the
    `*List()` accessors (`SourceAddressList`, `DestinationAddressList`,
    `DestinationPortList` added beside the existing name/application/
    protocol lists) instead of the scalar back-compat fields or
    open-coded plural-with-scalar-fallback copies (#16143). The
    destination-NAT compile diagnostics in particular previously saw
    only the first value of a bracket-list rule — a second invalid
    destination, unknown address-book name, or unknown application
    warned nowhere — and now validate and log every value. No scalar
    field or compiler back-fill write is removed: scalar-only matches
    (e.g. from an older peer) still resolve through the same accessors,
    and the marshalled config is unchanged.

  * Config: CoS loss-priority, scheduler priority, and tunnel mode
    are now typed string values with named constants (#16141). The
    wire tokens and empty-string defaults are unchanged; only the Go
    types moved, so a mistyped priority or mode is a compile error
    instead of a token that travels to the dataplane.

  * Config: `services security-intelligence`, `services
    advanced-anti-malware`, `services ssl initiation`/`termination`
    profiles, and `services application-identification` children now
    commit with a child-named accepted-only warning (#16079). xpf has
    no security-intelligence, anti-malware/ETI, or L7 AppID engine
    behind these stanzas, so they previously committed silently inert
    (only a generic no-L7 warning fired); each configured profile or
    child is now named in the warning, matching the SSL-proxy sibling
    (#10984). The stanzas remain loadable and preserved for display.
  * Login: `system login user root authentication …` now warns at
    commit instead of compiling silently while inert (#16076). The
    daemon never applies root credentials from a `login user` stanza;
    root's password and SSH keys come only from
    `system root-authentication`, which the warning names. The
    misleading docs claim that `user root authentication ssh-…`
    gives root a key is corrected. `user root class` remains honoured
    and does not warn.

  * Login: credential configuration paths are now priced above
    `configure` (#16080). A login class holding PermConfig without
    PermAll (e.g. a custom `[view configure]` class) is denied by name
    when mutating `system login`, `system root-authentication`, or
    `system services web-management api-auth` (plus the legacy
    `system services rest api-auth` spelling) on the CLI, gRPC, and
    REST surfaces — including load, group-expanded, embedded
    change-configuration, override, and rollback forms — even when it
    authored no *-configuration regexes or its regexes would allow the
    path. Only a super-user (all) class may rewrite login/root/api
    credentials, and the commit advisory for a configure-without-
    super-user class now names that fence.
  * User-identification: `show services user-identification status` and
    `show security user-identification status` (CLI, remote CLI, gRPC and
    REST) now mark every configured user-identification stanza INERT
    (accepted-only; no runtime effect) via one shared renderer (#16078).
    The stanzas commit with only a warning while their credentials are
    stored and no engine consumes them; previously they displayed as
    ordinary configuration, so an operator could believe identity-based
    policy was enforced.
  * Alarms: an unsupported AAA stanza (radius-server, tacplus-server,
    authentication-order, accounting) admitted by the tolerant load /
    peer-sync path now stays visible in `show system alarms` and
    `show security alarms` until the stanza is removed (#16077).
    Previously the local-only-authentication warning existed only as a
    boot-time log line, so a legacy persisted or peer-synced AAA tree
    ran local-only auth with no persistent signal after boot. Strict
    commit still rejects the stanza outright.
  * Login: the in-process console shell now re-resolves its user's
    `system login` class when the stanza changes, instead of keeping
    the assignment resolved once at daemon start (#16071). A console
    user who is re-classed or removed loses (or gains) the class on
    their next command without a daemon restart; a first `system
    login` stanza appearing likewise takes effect, and removing the
    stanza returns the shell to the unset legacy no-RBAC mode.
  * Gate commit-check on the config-lock holder on both the gRPC and REST
    surfaces: a non-holder no longer receives compile diagnostics about
    another session's staged candidate, and concurrent commit-checks are
    bounded to a single full compile (busy callers get a retryable
    ResourceExhausted / 429 instead of queueing compiles) (#16070).
  * Login: the tolerant-load shadowed-class neutralisation (#14387) now
    also covers `system services web-management api-auth` users, named
    keys, and the api-auth default class (#16074). A custom class
    shadowing a built-in (e.g. `class super-user { permissions view; }`)
    is inert at runtime, so an api-auth credential naming the shadowed
    class kept the built-in's permissions on tolerant load / peer sync;
    it is now neutralised to `unauthorized` with a warning, exactly like
    `system login user` assignments. Strict commit still rejects the
    shadowing definition.

  * REST config sessions: a session token is now bound to the principal
    that minted it (#16069). POST /config/enter records the authorized
    principal and the store holder epoch alongside the token, and every
    holder-scoped follow-up (set/delete/activate/deactivate/load/
    rollback/annotate/commit/commit-confirmed/confirm/exit and
    re-enter) must present the same principal; a stolen token presented
    by another equally-privileged principal is denied with 403 before
    the store is called. Enter and exit hold the binding lock across
    the store holder transition and the bind/unbind is checked against
    the recorded generation, so a concurrent same-token re-entry
    cannot have its binding erased by a stale exit. The owner's own
    workflow and store-entered (CLI/gRPC/internal) sessions are
    unchanged.
  * Login: gRPC Complete admission now canonicalises abbreviated
    operational words before judging allow/deny-commands (#16073), so
    `sh conf` / `sh sec pol` / `sh ver` are priced as `show
    configuration` / `show security policies` / `show version` exactly
    as the completion data-source gate already did; anchored denies
    fire on abbreviations and allowlisted abbreviations stay admitted.
    Config-mode `set …` lines keep their raw form.
  * Login: argument-scoped allow/deny-commands regexes now fire on the
    gRPC and REST surfaces (#16067). Both gates rebuild the full
    canonicalised command from the decoded request (ShowText topic
    parameters, Ping/Traceroute targets, session and match-policies
    selectors, ShowText filters) and judge it with the argument-free
    charge, so `^show route table secret-vrf`, `^ping 10\.99\.` and
    `^show log .*` deny remotely exactly as on the box; a request whose
    arguments cannot be reconstructed denies for a restricted class.
    REST show-text topics now resolve through the same longest-prefix
    rule as gRPC. The commit-time unenforceability advisory and the
    once-per-class runtime warning now consider argument-bearing
    spellings via per-surface registered argument samples, so an
    argument-scoped restriction that fires remotely is no longer
    reported as on-box-only.
  * NAT: pre-routing NAT no longer translates under the fabric
    fallback identity for ambiguous (unpromoted) fabric arrivals
    (#16045). Static/pool DNAT, persistent-NAT, NPTv6 and NAT64 were
    judged under the local fabric/logical zone while the later policy
    promoted the claimed zone, so a fabric-scoped rule fired for a
    zone pair that never selected it. Scoped matchers now fail closed
    under ambiguous provenance (pool DNAT poison-drop, static/NPTv6/
    NAT64 withheld, persistent reader skipped); unscoped rules and
    native owner-scope ingress are unchanged, and the claimed zone is
    never granted before owner-RG proof.
  * Drop IPv6 packets in the AF_XDP shim when their extension-header chain
    exceeds the walk limit, instead of passing an unresolved protocol with
    zero ports to the kernel for local, multicast, or link-local
    destinations. Resolvable chains are unaffected (#16038).
  * PPTP ALG: the GRE data-channel resolve now looks associations up
    in the routing domain of the claimed fabric identity the flow is
    re-keyed to (validated or ambiguous zone), not the fabric link's
    own ingress domain (#16055). On an ambiguous fabric arrival the
    same call ID in another tenant's domain is no longer claimed or
    idle-refreshed, and a fabric arrival carrying no claimed zone
    resolves in domain 0 (fail closed) rather than the fabric link's
    domain.
  * Cap the cluster bulk-sync receive window at the forward-session table
    capacity and expire a window held open past an hour. The cap is on
    live occupancy (a delete inside the window frees its slot), the
    deadline is enforced on receive, on the receive-loop read-timeout
    tick (so a silent peer still expires), and at BulkEnd, and an
    aborted window latches bulk-incomplete readiness debt until a later
    bulk reconciles instead of clearing the failover blocker. Distinct
    keys past the cap are counted and dropped without storing; the
    transient-failure marker sets share the cap. New counters
    BulkRecvCapped and BulkRecvAborted render in cluster status when
    nonzero (#16039).
  * PPTP ALG: control-segment teaching now requires an in-window TCP
    data sequence, judged against the pre-note tracked state
    (#16037). An admitted but out-of-window Outgoing-Call-Reply no
    longer installs attacker-chosen call IDs, and a synced session
    with no sequence state learns nothing. `no-sequence-check`
    opts out, as for closing segments.
  * Cluster: session-sync peer liveness now refreshes only on
    semantically accepted frames, and every sync message type carries a
    per-type payload cap (#16041). Well-framed but rejected frames
    (unknown types, truncated records, stale full-sets, impossible
    clock readings) previously reset the missed-heartbeat count and the
    last-receive stamp before dispatch, so a faulty keyed peer could
    mask its own failover trigger indefinitely. Over-cap headers are
    refused before the payload is read, counted as
    OversizeFramesRefused in cluster statistics, and drop the
    connection; unknown types remain globally capped and ignored for
    forward compatibility.
  * Sessions: an in-window but non-exact RST no longer stamps session
    teardown (#16040). The closing-segment sequence verdict now
    requires an exact sequence match when the RST bit is set (RFC 9293
    §3.5.3 / RFC 5961 §3.2), compared against the endpoint's tracked
    receive-next (learned from the peer direction's ACK field, so
    downstream-loss recovery RSTs still close) rather than the
    firewall-observed send-end — closing the ~window-size blind-guess
    teardown left by #15947 leg1. FIN keeps receive-window validity,
    `no-sequence-check` still bypasses the verdict, and entries with
    no tracked sequence/ACK state are still admitted.

  * NAT: freed PAT ports on small source pools (<=256 ports per
    address) now sit out a 60 s minimum reuse delay before any new
    flow can claim them (#16035). Previously the occupancy bit cleared
    and the port recycled FIFO with no quarantine, so on a tiny or
    high-churn pool a delayed UDP reply reusing the old
    (translated address, port, remote) tuple could be delivered to a
    new owner of the same port. Large pools are unchanged (their
    FIFO-after-fresh-range reuse already spaces reissue beyond the
    delay), and no protocol is exempted at the allocator layer.
    Fold: repeat frees refresh the token's stamp to the latest free,
    the keyed probe runs under the recycle mutex so a concurrent free
    cannot race its quarantine snapshot, persistent-clear and
    short-timeout GC frees are stamped like flow frees, specific-port
    reserves honour the delay (a synced flow re-joining its own
    untombstoned lease on the same port is exempt), and collision
    requeues restore the dedup bit so no duplicate token can form.
  * NAT: interface SNAT can no longer mint on a static-NAT external
    (#16034). Strict commit already rejected the pair; the Rust
    dataplane now refuses such interface mints (address-only and PAT,
    including after an attempted PAT) and dynamic HA-synced interface
    imports with InterfaceIdentityExhausted on tolerated installs
    (lenient load / peer-sync), where the collision previously
    published a duplicate wire tuple. A port-mapped static is PAT'd
    around and refused only on its exact port; genuine static imports
    keep their no-reservation success. The NPTv6 leg was #16015.
  * Sessions: handshake promotion/completion now validates the TCP ack
    number (#16042). A SYN-ACK or completing ACK whose ack does not equal
    the companion direction's SYN ack end (ISN + 1 + SYN payload,
    wrapping) no longer promotes a session off the opening window at any
    gate — lookup, flow-cache hit, keepalive, or HA update/promote. Each
    direction's ack end is learned from its SYN/SYN-ACK (Owner-admitted
    notes only, so a rejected SYN-ACK cannot launder evidence) or seeded
    at install (both halves, through the NAT reverse key, so translated
    pairs validate identically). Companion resolution runs only for
    handshake-pending pairs. Unknown evidence (mid-stream pickup, HA
    import, unparsed ack) fails open on the opening window, so a
    failover import's first completing segment still completes.
  * Cluster: an open control-key rotation now ages visibly and alarms
    when forgotten (#16029). UpdateConfig stamps when the current
    additional-authentication-key window opened (keyed on the additional
    key's identity, so re-applying the identical config cannot launder
    the age; swapping the additional key re-anchors, finalize clears).
    `show chassis cluster statistics` renders the window's age on the
    Key rotation line, and past 7 days the line carries an ALARM naming
    the window by key ID — the retired key is still accepted, so a
    forgotten step-5 finalize previously left it valid indefinitely.
    The alarm never auto-finalizes (retiring a key the peer still signs
    with reopens the dual-master window); the stamp is in-memory and
    re-anchors at the first apply after a daemon restart, so a stale
    alarm can be up to one restart late, never early.
  * Logging: unify the raw-event wire parser behind logEvent and
    DecodeRawEventRecord (#16127). parseRawEvent is now the single
    offset parser (zero-allocation, caller-owned rawEvent) and
    formatRawEventAddrs the single address renderer, so the live and
    decode-only paths cannot diverge on offsets or family rendering.
    Unknown-address-family policy is explicit and pinned: only
    AF_INET (2) / AF_INET6 (10) are accepted — the decoder rejects and
    the live path drops any other family byte, replacing the former
    live-only "non-v6 means IPv4" fallback. Golden short / legacy-144 /
    extended / IPv6 frames pin live-vs-decoded parity. Fanout is
    unchanged (parser-only unification).
  * Fabric: GetSessions is no longer admitted tokenless during the
    rollout grace (#16033). A keyed node whose peer has never
    authenticated previously grace-admitted GetSessions for up to five
    minutes per anchor event, exposing the full session table (complete
    5-tuples, NAT bindings and counters) to any on-segment host with no
    token in peer-down, replacement and rollout windows. GetSessions now
    requires a valid fabric token at all times; GetStatus,
    GetSessionSummary, GetZonePairSummary, MonitorInterface and the
    fabric-safe ShowText topic (counts/statistics only) remain
    grace-eligible, and a keyed peer's valid-token GetSessions proxy is
    unchanged.
  * Fabric: legacy method-only MonitorInterface streams are now rejected
    and open fabric streams re-authenticate continuously (#16031).
    Fabric streams must carry the args-bound token matching the request
    (missing/mismatched is Unauthenticated); the counted fallback and
    xpf_fabric_stream_args_unbound_total are removed. Local loopback
    monitor streams are unaffected. Open fabric streams re-verify every
    5s that the stream token's minting key is still in the accepted-key
    set (checked at the token's captured mint window, so window expiry
    alone never kills a healthy stream): removing the cluster PSK ends
    a stream within one tick, while a rotation still accepting the old
    key does not.
  * userspace-dp: GRE/WG shared outer-fragment pool can no longer be
    starved by tail fragments or one peer source (#16028). Pool state
    creation is now head-only: a GRE head creates an entry only after
    the full tunnel-endpoint matcher (GRE key, transport domain,
    resolved transport egress, ingress host-inbound/zone admission)
    passes, a WG head only after its listener/zone/ownership gates,
    and tails consult the pool without ever creating state. One outer
    source may hold at most half the 64-entry shared pool (no eviction
    of other sources), so a spoofed-peer tail/head flood cannot starve
    WireGuard or other GRE peers. Trade-off: a tail arriving before
    its head is now dropped instead of buffered (pre-head tail
    buffering was the starvation vector); correctly ordered fragmented
    tunnel traffic is unaffected. Fold: head-admission Key reads are
    bounded by the declared outer length (never trailing capture);
    probe counter charges reuse a per-thread scratch store (no
    per-head allocation); consult-only tail drops are counted in the
    pool drop counter. Accepted restriction, tested: a KEYED GRE
    header split across the first fragments (Key bytes beyond the
    head fragment) is refused at the head — the Key cannot be
    verified there; unkeyed (K-clear) split headers reassemble
    normally. Fold 2: a K-present split head is refused even against
    an unkeyed endpoint — the matcher can never accept K-present for
    a key-0 endpoint, so admitting it held guaranteed-dead state.

xpf (0.0.0) unstable; urgency=medium

  * Cluster: heartbeat anti-replay latch now survives a daemon restart
    (#16022). The peer epoch-capable latch, boot-epoch floor and its
    bound sessions are persisted in a generation-ordered, key-set-scoped
    record alongside ha-boot-epoch. Admission queues one coalescing
    asynchronous persistence worker; heartbeat and shutdown paths never
    perform or join filesystem I/O, and guarded writes skip if a newer
    generation is already on disk. Pre-#16022 a receiver restart cleared
    the latch/floor, and 65 captured sender
    incarnations replayed while the peer was down churned the 64-entry
    ring indefinitely (1625/1625 epochless admitted); the restored latch
    refuses the epochless set and the restored floor+bindings bound the
    epoch-bearing one to one finite ascending pass. The record is scoped
    to the accepted-key-set digest, so rotating the control-link PSK
    clears it on the next apply — the rollback recovery is now the
    rotation alone, with no state file to hand-edit; a failed or wedged
    write fails open to the previous process-scoped behaviour. Rollback
    recovery text, NoteEpochDowngradeHeartbeat and README residuals
    updated accordingly.

xpf (0.0.0) unstable; urgency=medium

  * Sessions: pressure shedding is protocol-symmetric and keyed on
    never-replied state (#16019). Rotating spoofed-source UDP/ICMP
    singles previously filled the session table while only a TCP
    initial-SYN newcomer could shed handshake-incomplete victims;
    now any qualifying new flow (TCP initial SYN, or any non-TCP first
    packet) at the 90% watermark sheds unreplied local sessions of
    any protocol, and replied/established flows are never victims.
    Fold: victim reply evidence is node-level (shared-map reverse
    row rev counter > 0 + peer-synced fence), not just the shedding
    worker's counters or reverse-row existence; retained victims have
    their queued Close delta cancelled and deferred sheds recheck the
    reply predicate; the stale-candidate scan per admission is bounded.
    Fold 3: the reverse shared row itself is stamped with the forward
    entry's rev counter at publish/promote/reverse-install, so the
    cross-worker proof is readable on the row the check consults.
  * NAT: persistent-NAT permit tightening now revokes stored
    any-remote grants on allocator reuse (#16017). A reused pool
    allocator stamps its any-remote buckets with a permit version;
    tightening to target-host/target-host-port on rebuild leaves the
    stamp stale so inbound lookup denies non-target remotes, while
    an unchanged any-remote permit restamps and preserves the grant.
    Exact target-host/port grants remain permitted under tighter modes.
  * NAT: pool/interface SNAT can no longer mint inside an NPTv6
    external prefix (#16015). Strict commit rejects a referenced
    source-pool / NAT64-pool / interface-egress IPv6 member inside an
    installed NPTv6 external prefix (lenient load warns); the Rust
    dataplane refuses such pool, interface, and HA-synced mints with
    PoolPeerAddressOverlap. NPTv6 publishes the prefix statelessly
    with the port unchanged, so the minted tuple previously collided
    with the NPTv6-published tuple for a different host.


xpf (0.0.0) unstable; urgency=medium
  * Policy: a NoRoute flow (no userspace FIB egress) now adjudicates
    the known ingress's exact-pair deny/reject rules (deny-only;
    exact-pair permits stay delegated) and the egress-unconstrained
    tiers — `to-zone any`, both-any, and `junos-global` with an
    `Any` to-zone scope — before the default policy decides, so under
    `default-policy permit-all` an explicit matching deny/reject
    (including `trust->untrust deny`) drops the packet instead of
    delegating it to the kernel FIB (#16021). `from-zone any
    to-zone <z>` and to-zone-scoped globals stay ineligible without
    an egress zone, and an unmatched NoRoute flow still follows the
    default policy.

  * Fold (#16350): production PAT allocation now skips peer-owned
    candidates during the keyed probe (bounded by addresses x range,
    fail-closed with PoolPeerAddressOverlap on exclusion-exhaustion)
    instead of mint/rollback retrying the same port; NAT64 gained the
    same exclusion entry point, and the keyed helpers compile in
    production builds (not only under cfg(test)).


xpf (0.0.0) unstable; urgency=medium

  * NAT PAT port allocation is no longer predictable (#16011,
    RFC 6056): pool PAT claims, the interface-mode PAT walk and the
    stateless try-next-port path now start from a keyed hash of the
    flow tuple under a per-boot secret drawn from the OS CSPRNG, with
    a bounded fail-closed probe; the monotonic sequential cursor is
    removed. Deterministic CGNAT block allocation keeps its
    documented lowest-free-in-block contract, and freed-port recycle
    remains FIFO-queued.


xpf (0.0.0) unstable; urgency=medium

  * Screen: tolerant bad numerics no longer zero syn-flood subfields or
    limit-session (#16006, A3-M3). A bad syn-flood
    alarm/source/destination-threshold or timeout now keeps its Junos
    default (512/4000/4000/20) on the tolerant load/peer-sync path
    instead of falling to disabled behind a warning; a bad limit-session
    value has no default, so the profile is treated as unresolved and
    referencing zones get the substituted conservative default
    (malformed-packet checks only) rather than the profile with a cap
    silently off — preserving any authored alarm-without-drop modifier
    through that unresolved path so a substituted drop is logged and
    forwarded, not enforced. Strict commit still rejects every bad
    value.
  * NAT: persistent leases are now charged per source (#16013). The
    pool allocator refuses a NEW persistent lease once one
    (src_ip, routing_scope) holds 4096 leases (live or idle-retained),
    on the local PAT and address-only paths, HA-synced reserves,
    idle-lease imports, and deterministic NAT64 BIBs; joining an
    existing lease is unaffected. Previously only the pool-global cap
    applied, so one host's open/close churn could retain the whole
    lease table (and a single-address pool's ports) for the 300s-24h
    idle timeout while other sources were refused.


